**assistant**:
> Splitting on commas with `getline(..., ',')` is not CSV-safe: commas inside quoted fields shift all later columns.
> 
> For proper CSV such as:
> 
> ```csv
> name,lat,lon
> "COLLINSTON, UT",41.82,-112.06
> ```
> 
> use a quote-aware parser. A small replacement for your line splitting is:
> 
> ```cpp
> std::vector<std::string> parseCsvLine(const std::string& line) {
>     std::vector<std::string> fields;
>     std::string field;
>     bool quoted = false;
> 
>     for (size_t i = 0; i < line.size(); ++i) {
>         char c = line[i];
> 
>         if (c == '"') {
>             // RFC-style escaped quote: ""
>             if (quoted && i + 1 < line.size() && line[i + 1] == '"') {
>                 field += '"';
>                 ++i;
>             } else {
>                 quoted = !quoted;
>             }
>         } else if (c == ',' && !quoted) {
>             fields.push_back(field);
>             field.clear();
>         } else {
>             field += c;
>         }
>     }
> 
>     if (quoted)
>         throw std::runtime_error("Unclosed quote in CSV row.");
> 
>     fields.push_back(field);
>     return fields;
> }
> ```
> 
> Then replace both parsing loops:
> 
> ```cpp
> auto headers = parseCsvLine(line);
> for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(headers.size()); ++i) {
>     std::string& header = headers[i];
>     header.erase(std::remove_if(header.begin(), header.end(),
>                                 [](unsigned char c) { return std::isspace(c); }),
>                  header.end());
> 
>     if (header == "lat") latIdx = i;
>     if (header == "lon") lonIdx = i;
> }
> ```
> 
> And for each data row:
> 
> ```cpp
> auto fields = parseCsvLine(line);
> 
> if (latIdx < static_cast<int>(fields.size()) &&
>     lonIdx < static_cast<int>(fields.size())) {
>     nodes.push_back(new HydroOutputNode(
>         std::stod(fields[latIdx]),
>         std::stod(fields[lonIdx])
>     ));
> }
> ```
> 
> Also check that the file opened:
> 
> ```cpp
> std::ifstream file(filepath);
> if (!file)
>     throw std::runtime_error("Could not open: " + filepath);
> ```
> 
> For production use, I would favor a library such as **csv-parser** (`vincentlaucsb/csv-parser`), particularly if fields may contain embedded newlines. The helper above handles quoted commas and doubled quotes, but not multiline quoted CSV fields.
> 
> Incidentally, a normal quoted station name is `"COLLINSTON, UT"`. The literal `""COLLINSTON, UT"` appears malformed unless it is an escaped representation from another format.
